A||N||5||87SC/GT||How big is to big? (wheels & tires)||||5||
Z||000000||87SC/GT||02-26-2000||10:29 PM||||I have officially begun the 3800 SC fiero project and while I wait for the motor to arrive, am shopping for tires and wheels.<BR>My Gt is dropped 1.5-2" . Anyone running 17" wheels with a 2" drop? Will you give away the magic combination? backspace/width/tire size?<BR>Thanks.<P>**** EDIT: Make that "How big is TOO big"<BR>I is a produk ov Iowa Edukacionel sistum.<p>[This message has been edited by 87SC/GT (edited 02-26-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-27-2000||01:07 AM||||Awfully specific question.  Here's a really general answer.<BR>To maintain original geometry add .5 inches backspace for every 1 inch rim width addition.  Maintain original diameter, tire sites have tables to use.  Keep tread width and rim width reasonably close.<P>For specific modifications get a Suspension Person familiar with Fieros to work the #'s specific to your ride.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||02-27-2000||01:22 AM||||Ran 17x7 42mm offset front, 215/40 17x8.5 40mm offset rear 255/40 (didn't scrub but killed in fight with road hazzard then changed to 245/40) Have 4 18x8 now. no tires but the fronts on their way 235/35/18 Yokohama AVS Sports. The rear are gunna be 255/35 AVS also... ran out of money though... Not sure of back spacing will check paper work form  <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.discounttiredirect.com</A>   pic of my car on 17s <A HREF="http://www.freeyellow.com/members/fiero/travis.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.freeyellow.com/members/fiero/travis.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||87SC/GT||02-27-2000||01:23 AM||||Phaeton, Thanks for the reply. That makes sense. V8 Archie had the famous blue chop top  GT with a 2" drop and 17's but he's not talkin'. Unfortunately I am geographically challenged and will have to mail order my tire/wheel combo. I am also thinking of switching to coilovers when I put in the 3800SC, that should allow more tire/wheel. Thanks, J||||reg||1
